[USA_AFR.FullName]
Flaming "Torch"
[USA_AFR.Description]


[USA_AFR.Epilogue]
After defeating the Italo-German forces in Africa, Lieutenant Colonel Roger Waters got back home in March 1943. In May 1943 he was promoted to Colonel and transferred to teaching again. He retired in 1973.

‘Not a smart, who knows how to distinguish good from evil. Smart is who knows how to choose the lesser of two evils’. Al-Harizi

[Faid.FullName]
Beyond the Faid Pass
[Faid.Description]
\t On February 14, 1943, the Germans launched Operation Frühlingswind to force the Allies to retreat outside the borders of Tunisia. The day before the Allies intercepted and deciphered an enemy radiogram with information about the coming offensive. Thus, the 1st Army HQ was ordered to set its troops to full alert and expect the attack. The only thing they didn't know was the main attack axis. High command believed that it would come from Fondouk, other attacks being feints. The day before, Eisenhower arrived to inspect the troops. He was disappointed with the state of the defenses at this sector of the front. Most officers had no idea about even the most basic laws of tactics, not one of them knew how to establish defensive positions or where to lay mines. Many soldiers didn't even know how to shoot. Eisenhower believed that the unit disposition of the 1st Armored Division was not correct. The units didn't cover each other and would even be in each other's way if the enemy acted in a different way from what the division commanders assumed.
\t Lieutenant Colonel Waters was ordered to stop the German attack on Faid pass. His combat team mainly consisted of Stuart light tanks, infantry and anti-tank guns. Roger Waters was a young officer. Before he enlisted in the mid-30s, he had worked as a schoolteacher in Seattle. Roger was born on March 31, 1907 as the only child. His father was a priest in the local church, and his mother was a schoolteacher. Since childhood little Roger had dreamed about becoming a pilot, but the fate decreed otherwise. Having graduated from the college with honors, he continued his education in the Massachusetts University. After graduating, he returned to Seattle and became a schoolteacher. At the same time he got married and in a year a son was born to him. Money was scarce. Once, he saw an army recruitment ad and enlisted. Having served a year as a private, he was sent to a military academy. Having graduated, he was promoted to Lieutenant and assigned to armored forces for further service. His career was further propelled by his lively and capacious mind as well as his ability to get along with subordinates who never questioned his orders. Whichever unit he was put in charge of, it was always one of the best in combat training and discipline. Right before the war he was sent to a military academy to teach. When the question of redeploying U.S. troops to Africa had arisen, it turned out that competent commanders were scarce. It was decided to call up the best military academy teachers, Lieutenant Colonel Roger Waters included. In November 1942, his armored regiment accepted its first battle. Only thanks to competent leadership they suffered but little casualties. However, if that time they chased Rommel's forces, this time they had to defend. By February 14, Waters's team was one of the most experienced in the U.S. forces, as over half of its personnel could be considered veterans. 

[SidiBouZid.FullName]
The Battle of Sidi Bou Zid
[SidiBouZid.Description]
\t By the evening of February 14, 1943, the Germans managed to crush U.S. defenses at the village of Sidi Bou Zid. Eisenhower's gloomiest concerns were fulfilled. Casualties were too heavy, a result of the inexperience of the recruits. The German forces made the most out of these favorable circumstances and delivered blows to the most vulnerable sectors of the front. Eisenhower's troops wavered and retreated. Most American soldiers saw German tanks for the first time, feeling complety helpless and terrorized. They watched friendly tanks being destroyed one after another, while enemy vehicles advanced with nearly no casualties. Many sectors were abandoned in panic, but some units courageously blunted the attacks. The forces were unequal and sometimes U.S. units fought to the last soldier. Waters's team was one of those. Competent cooperation of different arms and competent defenses were factors that helped to avoid huge casualties. Still, in the evening of February 14, Waters was ordered to retreat. This came just in time, as in a while his team would have been surrounded. The Colonel's unit was also lucky that the Germans weakened their frontal attack, having diverted their focus on the flanks. The night of February 14/15 was spent in preparations of new defenses. Waters ordered to establish positions for isolated fighting. At night he contacted Army HQ. The Chief of Staff passed along Eisenhower's order to counter-attack German forces at Sidi Bou Zid and win back recently lost positions in the morning of February 15. Waters replied that he had not enough troops and military hardware for that. He was told to take command of Alger's Armored Battalion equipped with the newest Sherman tanks. High command believed that putting such powerful vehicles into the field would enable U.S. forces to easily hurl back the enemy beyond the Faid pass. But they forgot that Alger's Battalion mainly consisted of greenhorns. In the morning of February 15, the battalion started to advance towards Sidi Bou Zid.

[Sbeitla.FullName]
The Battle of Sbeitla
[Sbeitla.Description]
\t The counter-attack was a failure. U.S. forces were forced to retreat to Sbeitla, suffering even more casualties. It seemed that an utter defeat is at hand. But fate intervened. German high command represented by von Arnim dared not to chase the Allied forces, against the wishes of Rommel who insisted on immediate exploitation of success. As a result, the U.S. forces were given a whole 24 hours. During this time, they established defences in Sbeitla and fully reorganized their troops. Thus, when the German tanks set out for this town, they were already opposed by rather solid defenses. 
\t Roger Waters always tried to inquire deeper into matters he dealt with. In the academy he thoroughly studied materials on armored forces, including new theories. Being a teacher, he studied the works of Manstein and Guderian and used to tell his students about possible enemy actions in the war to come. This time he could use this knowledge himself. Although Lieutenant Colonel Waters spent the entire day of February 16 establishing positions, he had enough time to consider the disposition of the coming battle. He tried to think as Rommel to understand what the enemy would do. Waters realized that Rommel wouldn’t be sitting out in defense, but would attack to capture Sbeitla by noon. Most likely, the enemy would again try to outflank instead of making a frontal attack. So he decided to wait for the attack and then, having repelled the main strike, to counter-attack with Alger's Armored Battalion. The plan was good. But would they manage to blunt Rommel's offensive? That surely wasn't the easiest question for Waters.

[Kasserine.FullName]
The Kasserine Pass
[Kasserine.Description]
\t Lieutenant Colonel Waters and his team managed to blunt the German attack at Sbeitla and even staged a counter-attack. But the general situation on the entire sector of the front was not beneficial for the Allies. For this reason, Waters's team was ordered to retreat to the Kasserine pass. Despite general failures of Allied forces, Waters's team could rightfully be considered victorious, as their heroic resistance helped the main body to retreat with little casualties. Besides, the soldiers realized that the enemy could be defeated and that raised their morale even more. 
\t The Allies were again lucky. The high commands of the Italian and German forces were still in disagreement. Rommel insisted on advancing to Tebessa in the northwest to cut Allied supply lines. He believed that after such a strike the Allies would be forced to withdraw the main forces to Algeria. But von Arnim thought quite differently. Rommel was annoyed with his excessive caution and offered the same plan to the Italian HQ. They agreed, but wanted to strike at Thala and Le Kef to the north, instead of the northwest. Rommel considered this extreme lack of foresight, but obeyed and devised a new plan of attack. All this was beneficial for the U.S. troops, as they were again given time to prepare. Besides, Eisenhower ordered to put fresh forces into the field. As a result, the Allies gained significant numerical superiority. The high command considered the Kasserine pass as a very favorable location to establish a defense. Narrow crossings between mountains could easily be swept by anti-tank gun fire. In the morning of February 19, Lieutenant Colonel Roger Waters was given Eisenhower's order to place infantry and artillery to the front to face the first German attacks. Then a British striking force was to come to help.

[Tebessa.FullName]
On the way to Tebessa
[Tebessa.Description]
\t The next day, on February 21, 1943, Lieutenant Colonel Waters was summoned to Division HQ for a meeting attended by Eisenhower. There a tactical plan was devised. In general, all commanders believed that the enemy was running out of power. This was confirmed by intelligence data of troop movements in the German rear. The enemy had nearly no reserves and the army, constantly on the march, was exhausted. At the end of the meeting, new assignments were made. To his surprise, Lieutenant Colonel Waters was put in charge of a combat team, a part of Combat Command B. In an hour, having packed his things, he was on the way to Tebessa. Right before setting out, he received some great news from home. A daughter had been born to him. For all the short time Roger had spent in Africa that was the most important news. The joy he felt outweighed all thoughts that harassed him since the meeting with Eisenhower. Away from home, where death stalked the fields, a birth of a new human being seemed something extraordinary. He realized he hadn't had such a deep feeling after the birth of his son. In civilian life a birth of another man is ordinary. But here... Suddenly he understood that he hated this war and all who started it. He couldn't realize now why people failed to agree and started sending innocent people to death. Before he had believed everything was right, evolving the only possible way. Now it seemed impossible that he was going to command people that in 24 hours would be killing other people who also had children and parents. For a spell, Roger Waters felt afraid. Having arrived, he shut out those gloomy thoughts and entered upon his duties. Lieutenant Colonel Waters was a true soldier. In the morning of February 22, he lead his men to battle.
